.SS " bool pip\&._internal\&.index\&.package_finder\&._check_link_requires_python (\fBLink\fP link, Tuple[int, int, int] version_info, bool  ignore_requires_python = \fCFalse\fP)\fC [protected]\fP"

.PP
.nf
Return whether the given Python version is compatible with a link's
'Requires-Python' value\&.

:param version_info: A 3-tuple of ints representing the Python
    major-minor-micro version to check\&.
:param ignore_requires_python: Whether to ignore the 'Requires-Python'
    value if the given Python version isn't compatible\&.

.fi
.PP
 
.SS " Optional[str] pip\&._internal\&.index\&.package_finder\&._extract_version_from_fragment (str fragment, str canonical_name)\fC [protected]\fP"

.PP
.nf
Parse the version string from a <package>+<version> filename
'fragment' (stem) or egg fragment\&.

:param fragment: The string to parse\&. E\&.g\&. foo-2\&.1
:param canonical_name: The canonicalized name of the package this
    belongs to\&.

.fi
.PP
 
.SS " int pip\&._internal\&.index\&.package_finder\&._find_name_version_sep (str fragment, str canonical_name)\fC [protected]\fP"

.PP
.nf
Find the separator's index based on the package's canonical name\&.

:param fragment: A <package>+<version> filename 'fragment' (stem) or
    egg fragment\&.
:param canonical_name: The package's canonical name\&.

This function is needed since the canonicalized name does not necessarily
have the same length as the egg info's name part\&. An example::

>>> fragment = 'foo__bar-1\&.0'
>>> canonical_name = 'foo-bar'
>>> _find_name_version_sep(fragment, canonical_name)
8

.fi
.PP
 
.SS " List[\fBInstallationCandidate\fP] pip\&._internal\&.index\&.package_finder\&.filter_unallowed_hashes (List[\fBInstallationCandidate\fP] candidates, Optional[\fBHashes\fP] hashes, str project_name)"

.PP
.nf
Filter out candidates whose hashes aren't allowed, and return a new
list of candidates\&.

If at least one candidate has an allowed hash, then all candidates with
either an allowed hash or no hash specified are returned\&.  Otherwise,
the given candidates are returned\&.

Including the candidates with no hash specified when there is a match
allows a warning to be logged if there is a more preferred candidate
with no hash specified\&.  Returning all candidates in the case of no
matches lets pip report the hash of the candidate that would otherwise
have been installed (e\&.g\&. permitting the user to more easily update
their requirements file with the desired hash)\&.

.fi
.PP
 
